{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6Xg1UbDCojHatw5x1v2St","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"multi-head attention heads pruning entropy importance \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One\" Voita \"Analyzing multi-head self-attention specialized heads\"\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.09418\"},{\"title\":\"are sixteen heads really better than one\",\"url\":\"https://aihub.org/2020/03/26/are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one\"},{\"title\":\"By Paul Michel\",\"url\":\"https://aihub.org/?p=1866\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09418\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\",\"url\":\"https://vimeo.com/385434677\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.08375\"},{\"title\":\"alphaxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://alphaxiv.org/overview/1905.10650v3\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\",\"url\":\"https://preview.aclanthology.org/update-py-deps/P19-1580/\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\",\"url\":\"https://preview.aclanthology.org/menus/P19-1580/\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\",\"url\":\"https://preview.aclanthology.org/setup/P19-1580\"}]\n\nThe search turned up both papers and some background on the pruning methods.\n\n**Michel, Levy & Neubig, \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\" (NeurIPS 2019)**\n- The method ranks heads with an importance score, estimated on a validation set or a slice of training data, and prunes low-scoring heads first. The score is the expected absolute change in loss when a head is removed. To address these issues, we turned to the various approaches explored in the pruning literature to compute an importance score, estimated on a validation set or a subset of the training data, to be used as a proxy for determining the order in which to prune heads. A low importance score means that head will be pruned first.\n- Ablating heads one at a time showed that very few had a noticeable effect. To our surprise, very few attention heads had any actual effect.\n- It is possible to reduce the number of heads by up to 60% without any loss in performance depending on the task and the model.\n- The authors also observed that during the first few epochs, the pruning profile is linear, which suggests that all heads are equally important.\n\n**Voita, Talbot, Moiseev, Sennrich & Titov, \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\" (ACL 2019)**\n- This paper studies encoder heads in neural machine translation. It uses a pruning method based on stochastic gates and a differentiable relaxation of the L0 penalty, rather than the entropy-based criterion your query mentions. When pruning heads using a method based on stochastic gates and a differentiable relaxation of the L0 penalty, we observe that specialized heads are last to be pruned.\n- Its headline result: on the English-Russian WMT dataset, pruning 38 out of 48 encoder heads results in a drop of only 0.15 BLEU.\n- The paper's most important and confident heads play consistent and often linguistically-interpretable roles.\n\n**Caveats**\n- None of the results I found mention entropy-based head importance. Confidence and entropy appear in Voita et al.'s framing, but the snippets I saw don't show an entropy score being used for pruning, so I can't confirm that connection.\n- An AlphaXiv overview page exists, but its content is generic and I wouldn't rely on it. The primary papers and Paul Michel's blog post are more reliable.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30402c77-19f2-456c-9b5e-2e376add52e6","uuid":"8ba55d6b-dc70-4bcc-ae9b-a1ef76e4d1ac","timestamp":"2026-10-31T01:59:27.325Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"multi-head attention heads pruning entropy importance \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One\" Voita \"Analyzing multi-head self-attention specialized heads\"","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wm9y6UWU7X4cFpS8Rieeu3","content":[{"title":"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.09418"},{"title":"are sixteen heads really better than one","url":"https://aihub.org/2020/03/26/are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one"},{"title":"By Paul Michel","url":"https://aihub.org/?p=1866"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09418"},{"title":"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned","url":"https://vimeo.com/385434677"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.08375"},{"title":"alphaxiv.org","url":"https://alphaxiv.org/overview/1905.10650v3"},{"title":"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned","url":"https://preview.aclanthology.org/update-py-deps/P19-1580/"},{"title":"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned","url":"https://preview.aclanthology.org/menus/P19-1580/"},{"title":"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned","url":"https://preview.aclanthology.org/setup/P19-1580"}]},"The search turned up both papers and some background on the pruning methods.\n\n**Michel, Levy & Neubig, \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\" (NeurIPS 2019)**\n- The method ranks heads with an importance score, estimated on a validation set or a slice of training data, and prunes low-scoring heads first. The score is the expected absolute change in loss when a head is removed. To address these issues, we turned to the various approaches explored in the pruning literature to compute an importance score, estimated on a validation set or a subset of the training data, to be used as a proxy for determining the order in which to prune heads. A low importance score means that head will be pruned first.\n- Ablating heads one at a time showed that very few had a noticeable effect. To our surprise, very few attention heads had any actual effect.\n- It is possible to reduce the number of heads by up to 60% without any loss in performance depending on the task and the model.\n- The authors also observed that during the first few epochs, the pruning profile is linear, which suggests that all heads are equally important.\n\n**Voita, Talbot, Moiseev, Sennrich & Titov, \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\" (ACL 2019)**\n- This paper studies encoder heads in neural machine translation. It uses a pruning method based on stochastic gates and a differentiable relaxation of the L0 penalty, rather than the entropy-based criterion your query mentions. When pruning heads using a method based on stochastic gates and a differentiable relaxation of the L0 penalty, we observe that specialized heads are last to be pruned.\n- Its headline result: on the English-Russian WMT dataset, pruning 38 out of 48 encoder heads results in a drop of only 0.15 BLEU.\n- The paper's most important and confident heads play consistent and often linguistically-interpretable roles.\n\n**Caveats**\n- None of the results I found mention entropy-based head importance. Confidence and entropy appear in Voita et al.'s framing, but the snippets I saw don't show an entropy score being used for pruning, so I can't confirm that connection.\n- An AlphaXiv overview page exists, but its content is generic and I wouldn't rely on it. The primary papers and Paul Michel's blog post are more reliable."],"durationSeconds":5.969188731000002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01N6Xg1UbDCojHatw5x1v2St","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B2GxF2Lm2NSFkxTkftqziz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"singular values spectrum of attention matrix low-rank during training transformer effective rank evolution self-attention\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"How Large Language Models Get Stuck: Early structure with persistent errors\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.00359\"},{\"title\":\"Dimensional Collapse in Transformer Attention Outputs: A Challenge for Sparse Dictionary Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.16929\"},{\"title\":\"Beyond Perplexity: A Geometric and Spectral Study of Low-Rank Pre-Training\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.13652\"},{\"title\":\"Linformer: Self-Attention with Linear Complexity\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.04768\"},{\"title\":\"web3.arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://web3.arxiv.org/pdf/2507.09394\"},{\"title\":\"Mind the Gap: a Spectral Analysis of Rank Collapse\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.07799v1\"},{\"title\":\"Alireza Naderi\",\"url\":\"https://www.catalyzex.com/author/Alireza Naderi\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.16929v3\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.07799v1\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07799v1\"}]\n\nThe search turned up a few relevant threads, though little that directly tracks attention-matrix effective rank across full training runs.\n\n- **Training-time tracking:** A 2026 preprint on LLM training dynamics reports that the effective rank of the four attention projections (W_Q, W_K, W_V, W_O) in a 12-layer causal transformer declined gradually over 30,000 steps. The effective rank, defined as the number of singular values needed to capture 99% of the spectral energy, decreases monotonically from approximately 590–592 at initialization to 550–575 by step 30,000, uniformly across all layers. The same paper notes that the smallest singular value does not collapse to zero in any layer or projection over the course of training. This is a single run, so it's a case study rather than a general result.\n- **Low-rank QK structure in pretrained models:** The Linformer paper found that the results exhibit a clear long-tail spectrum distribution across each layer, head and task, and that the spectrum distribution in higher layers is more skewed than in lower layers. This describes a pretrained model, not the trajectory during training.\n- **Training dynamics of QK spectra:** A New York University study uses Marchenko–Pastur diagnostics on the W_Q W_K^T Gram matrix throughout pretraining, with metrics including outlier count, MP-gap, and stable rank. It compares standard multi-head attention against multi-head latent attention. I only saw the setup, not the findings.\n- **Initialization:** Random-matrix analyses find that a spectral gap between the two largest singular values of the attention matrix drives rank collapse in width and in depth at initialization. This concerns the early dynamics of attention rather than later training.\n- **Metric definitions:** Effective rank is commonly defined as the exponential of the entropy of normalized singular values, following Roy & Vetterli (2007). Intuitively, the effective rank captures how evenly the singular values are distributed—higher values indicate a more isotropic spectrum, whereas lower values reflect concentration along a few dominant directions.\n\nA caveat: the \"effective rank\" definitions differ across papers. Some use an energy threshold (the 99% version above), others use entropy, so numbers aren't directly comparable. For a fuller picture of how attention spectra evolve during training, the NYU MP-diagnostics paper and the 2026 preprint are the most direct starting points.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30402c77-19f2-456c-9b5e-2e376add52e6","uuid":"73d2368d-3836-49d3-8f7b-e86b13a076a2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T01:59:29.369Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"singular values spectrum of attention matrix low-rank during training transformer effective rank evolution self-attention","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01SAMSCP8BkN4Hr7AeGPB8gW","content":[{"title":"How Large Language Models Get Stuck: Early structure with persistent errors","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.00359"},{"title":"Dimensional Collapse in Transformer Attention Outputs: A Challenge for Sparse Dictionary Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.16929"},{"title":"Beyond Perplexity: A Geometric and Spectral Study of Low-Rank Pre-Training","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.13652"},{"title":"Linformer: Self-Attention with Linear Complexity","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.04768"},{"title":"web3.arxiv.org","url":"https://web3.arxiv.org/pdf/2507.09394"},{"title":"Mind the Gap: a Spectral Analysis of Rank Collapse","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.07799v1"},{"title":"Alireza Naderi","url":"https://www.catalyzex.com/author/Alireza Naderi"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.16929v3"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.07799v1"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07799v1"}]},"The search turned up a few relevant threads, though little that directly tracks attention-matrix effective rank across full training runs.\n\n- **Training-time tracking:** A 2026 preprint on LLM training dynamics reports that the effective rank of the four attention projections (W_Q, W_K, W_V, W_O) in a 12-layer causal transformer declined gradually over 30,000 steps. The effective rank, defined as the number of singular values needed to capture 99% of the spectral energy, decreases monotonically from approximately 590–592 at initialization to 550–575 by step 30,000, uniformly across all layers. The same paper notes that the smallest singular value does not collapse to zero in any layer or projection over the course of training. This is a single run, so it's a case study rather than a general result.\n- **Low-rank QK structure in pretrained models:** The Linformer paper found that the results exhibit a clear long-tail spectrum distribution across each layer, head and task, and that the spectrum distribution in higher layers is more skewed than in lower layers. This describes a pretrained model, not the trajectory during training.\n- **Training dynamics of QK spectra:** A New York University study uses Marchenko–Pastur diagnostics on the W_Q W_K^T Gram matrix throughout pretraining, with metrics including outlier count, MP-gap, and stable rank. It compares standard multi-head attention against multi-head latent attention. I only saw the setup, not the findings.\n- **Initialization:** Random-matrix analyses find that a spectral gap between the two largest singular values of the attention matrix drives rank collapse in width and in depth at initialization. This concerns the early dynamics of attention rather than later training.\n- **Metric definitions:** Effective rank is commonly defined as the exponential of the entropy of normalized singular values, following Roy & Vetterli (2007). Intuitively, the effective rank captures how evenly the singular values are distributed—higher values indicate a more isotropic spectrum, whereas lower values reflect concentration along a few dominant directions.\n\nA caveat: the \"effective rank\" definitions differ across papers. Some use an energy threshold (the 99% version above), others use entropy, so numbers aren't directly comparable. For a fuller picture of how attention spectra evolve during training, the NYU MP-diagnostics paper and the 2026 preprint are the most direct starting points."],"durationSeconds":7.211916819999998,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01B2GxF2Lm2NSFkxTkftqziz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T728uCAtA3U8g3Apb4tLy1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Kolchinsky Tracey estimating mixture entropy pairwise distances; Saxe \"On the information bottleneck theory of deep learning\"; mutual information deterministic network infinite Goldfeld\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Information Bottleneck: Exact Analysis of (Quantized) Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.12912\"},{\"title\":\"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327\"},{\"title\":\"Information bottleneck method\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_bottleneck_method\"},{\"title\":\"The role of a layer in deep neural networks: a Gaussian Process perspective\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02354\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327v1\"},{\"title\":\"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26327v2\"},{\"title\":\"openreview.net\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf/b617eacd043db498a3da393c1fd34223f0f25f55.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"people.ece.cornell.edu\",\"url\":\"https://people.ece.cornell.edu/zivg/ICML2019.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26327v3\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327v3\"}]\n\nThe search returned material on the Saxe and Goldfeld side of this topic, but not the Kolchinsky and Tracey paper itself.\n\n- **Deterministic networks and infinite MI:** One paper notes that in deterministic DNNs with strictly monotone nonlinearities (e.g., tanh or sigmoid) the true mutual information I(X;T') is provably either infinite (continuous X) or a constant (discrete X). Goldfeld et al. (2019) is the source of this point, and another paper attributes the same issue to Saxe et al. (2018) and Amjad & Geiger (2019) (index 1-14).\n- **Kolchinsky and Tracey:** A Gaussian-process paper cites Saxe et al. and Kolchinsky & Tracey (2017) for the claim that in such settings mutual information quantities are ill defined and require a regulator. It also says the regulator is a noise scale ε, and that mutual information becomes a property of the pair-distribution-function (PDF) of the dataset, so compression can be studied through pairwise distances. I did not find the Kolchinsky and Tracey paper's own abstract, so the mixture-entropy details are unconfirmed.\n- **Saxe et al. (2019) \"On the information bottleneck theory of deep learning\":** One source says Saxe et al. presented counterexamples that challenge this view. In particular, the authors showed that compression phases depend critically on the activation function. Another says compression observed in tanh networks was not a genuine information-theoretic effect, but rather the result of injected randomness.\n- **Goldfeld et al. (2019) \"Estimating Information Flow in Deep Neural Networks\":** This paper argues that binned MI estimates are unreliable, and that even moderate coarse-graining can introduce substantial estimation error. It also proposes a stochastic-network estimator for MI.\n\nOverall, the sources agree that MI is ill-defined or trivial in deterministic continuous networks, and that binning-based measurements of compression are contested. A follow-up search for the Kolchinsky and Tracey mixture-entropy paper directly would be needed to confirm its specific method.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30402c77-19f2-456c-9b5e-2e376add52e6","uuid":"d62fbf98-0f1b-43f5-89a5-8cba91392602","timestamp":"2026-10-31T01:59:29.515Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Kolchinsky Tracey estimating mixture entropy pairwise distances; Saxe \"On the information bottleneck theory of deep learning\"; mutual information deterministic network infinite Goldfeld","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LkGgYkV5qcSywgLGK2vcgu","content":[{"title":"Information Bottleneck: Exact Analysis of (Quantized) Neural Networks","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.12912"},{"title":"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327"},{"title":"Information bottleneck method","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_bottleneck_method"},{"title":"The role of a layer in deep neural networks: a Gaussian Process perspective","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02354"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327v1"},{"title":"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26327v2"},{"title":"openreview.net","url":"https://openreview.net/pdf/b617eacd043db498a3da393c1fd34223f0f25f55.pdf"},{"title":"people.ece.cornell.edu","url":"https://people.ece.cornell.edu/zivg/ICML2019.pdf"},{"title":"A Generalized Information Bottleneck Theory of Deep Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26327v3"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.26327v3"}]},"The search returned material on the Saxe and Goldfeld side of this topic, but not the Kolchinsky and Tracey paper itself.\n\n- **Deterministic networks and infinite MI:** One paper notes that in deterministic DNNs with strictly monotone nonlinearities (e.g., tanh or sigmoid) the true mutual information I(X;T') is provably either infinite (continuous X) or a constant (discrete X). Goldfeld et al. (2019) is the source of this point, and another paper attributes the same issue to Saxe et al. (2018) and Amjad & Geiger (2019) (index 1-14).\n- **Kolchinsky and Tracey:** A Gaussian-process paper cites Saxe et al. and Kolchinsky & Tracey (2017) for the claim that in such settings mutual information quantities are ill defined and require a regulator. It also says the regulator is a noise scale ε, and that mutual information becomes a property of the pair-distribution-function (PDF) of the dataset, so compression can be studied through pairwise distances. I did not find the Kolchinsky and Tracey paper's own abstract, so the mixture-entropy details are unconfirmed.\n- **Saxe et al. (2019) \"On the information bottleneck theory of deep learning\":** One source says Saxe et al. presented counterexamples that challenge this view. In particular, the authors showed that compression phases depend critically on the activation function. Another says compression observed in tanh networks was not a genuine information-theoretic effect, but rather the result of injected randomness.\n- **Goldfeld et al. (2019) \"Estimating Information Flow in Deep Neural Networks\":** This paper argues that binned MI estimates are unreliable, and that even moderate coarse-graining can introduce substantial estimation error. It also proposes a stochastic-network estimator for MI.\n\nOverall, the sources agree that MI is ill-defined or trivial in deterministic continuous networks, and that binning-based measurements of compression are contested. A follow-up search for the Kolchinsky and Tracey mixture-entropy paper directly would be needed to confirm its specific method."],"durationSeconds":6.353149260999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01T728uCAtA3U8g3Apb4tLy1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
